            </button>
        </div>
    </header>

    <main class="w-full max-w-4xl flex-grow flex flex-col items-center">
        
        <!-- Learn Section -->
        <section id="section-learn" class="w-full glass-panel rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 w-full fade-in">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 text-slate-800 border-b pb-2" data-i18n="learnHeader">What are SI Prefixes?</h2>
            
            <div class="mb-8 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
                <p data-i18n="learnP1">
                    In the International System of Units (SI), <strong>prefixes</strong> are words or letters placed <span class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold">in front of basic units</span> (like meter, gram, second). They act as a <strong>multiplier (based on powers of 10)</strong> for that unit.
                </p>
                <p data-i18n="learnP2">
                    <strong>Why do we use prefixes?</strong> <br>
                    In science and daily life, we often work with very <em>large</em> numbers (like the distance to a star) or very <em>small</em> numbers (like the size of a virus). Writing many zeros is hard to read and takes too much space. Prefixes were created to make numbers shorter, easier to read, and standard around the world.
                </p>
                
                <div class="bg-indigo-50 border-l-4 border-indigo-500 p-5 rounded-r-lg shadow-sm">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-indigo-800 mb-2"><i class="fas fa-lightbulb text-yellow-500 mr-2"></i><span data-i18n="learnExTitle">Examples to help you understand:</span></h4>
                    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-sm md:text-base text-indigo-900 space-y-2">
                        <li data-i18n="learnEx1"><strong>Very large value:</strong> A distance of <strong>5,000 meters</strong> is equal to 5 &times; 10<sup>3</sup> meters. We use the prefix "kilo (10<sup>3</sup>)" instead, making it <strong>5 kilometers (5 km)</strong>.</li>
                        <li data-i18n="learnEx2"><strong>Very small value:</strong> A dust particle of <strong>0.000 001 meters</strong> is equal to 1 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> meters. We use the prefix "micro (10<sup>-6</sup>)" instead, making it <strong>1 micrometer (1 &mu;m)</strong>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
            
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-slate-800 border-b pb-2" data-i18n="tableHeader">Complete Table of SI Prefixes</h3>
            
            <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm mb-4">
                <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse prefix-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-700">
                            <th class="p-4 font-semibold border-b border-slate-200" data-i18n="colPrefix">Prefix</th>
                            <th class="p-4 font-semibold border-b border-slate-200" data-i18n="colSymbol">Symbol</th>
                            <th class="p-4 font-semibold border-b border-slate-200 text-right" data-i18n="colMultiplier">Multiplier</th>
                            <th class="p-4 font-semibold border-b border-slate-200" data-i18n="colPower">Power of 10</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody id="prefix-table-body" class="bg-white">
                        <!-- Table rows will be populated by JavaScript -->
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mt-6 bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-100 text-blue-800 text-sm" data-i18n="tipText">
                <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> Notice that common prefixes usually increase or decrease by steps of 10<sup>3</sup> (like kilo, mega, milli, micro).
            </div>
        </section>

